<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE HermitCrabInput SYSTEM "HermitCrabInput.dtd">
<!-- isActive breadth probe. edge-cases/loader-isactive already pins isActive on
     PhonologicalFeatureSystem, but the C# loader filters 25 DIFFERENT element collections through
     XmlLanguageLoader.IsActive, and covering one of them says nothing about the other 24: each
     .Where(IsActive) call site is its own opportunity to have been omitted. This grammar declares an
     INACTIVE decoy alongside the live declaration for every such collection reachable without a
     phonological feature system.

     Words that discriminate a decoy, named in words.yaml: Language (kul, which only parses because
     exactly one Language is active), SegmentDefinition (zal), MorphologicalSubrule (mukul),
     Allomorph (kol), LexicalEntry (mep), Stratum (qot), MorphologicalRule (qokul, via mrRuleOnly),
     Slot (qekul, via mrSlotOnly), AffixTemplate (qukul, via mrTemplateOnly/decoyTemplate), and
     BoundaryDefinition (kul= for bDecoy; mo+kul for bLive, which fails to LOAD rather than merely
     failing to parse once bLive is gone, since mrBoundaryPfx's own PhoneticShape "+" then names an
     undefined phoneme, equally conclusive evidence, per CounterfactualVerdict.RequiredToLoad).
     pekul is a conjunction over mrDecoy and slotDecoy alone (both stay jointly inactive on purpose,
     as the ORIGINAL, un-separable control) and does not itself separate those two call sites; mrDecoy
     and slotDecoy are why qokul/qekul had to be added as INDEPENDENTLY-reachable rule-only and
     slot-only controls instead of trying to split pekul apart.

     Four decoys (tableDecoy, ncDecoy, famDecoy, mprDecoy) are NOT discriminated by any SINGLE-flip
     counterfactual, and cannot be: XmlLanguageLoader.cs resolves CharacterDefinitionTable,
     FeatureNaturalClass, Family, and MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeature IDREFS through a direct
     Dictionary indexer (_tables[id], _natClasses[id], _families[id], _mprFeatures[id]), which throws
     when the id names an inactive (never-loaded) declaration. Flipping one of these four alone changes
     no result (nothing active references it); flipping a live reference to one alone throws at load
     (the target is still inactive). Each therefore carries its own dedicated, independently-inactive
     referencing partner (subNcDecoy, eTupDecoy, ePutDecoy, the TableDecoyStratum/eZDecoy pair) for
     CounterfactualGate.EvaluateJointly's three-run protocol: target alone, partner alone, both
     together. Slot and Stratum-level morphologicalRules IDREFS are the sole tolerant-lookup exception
     (Dictionary.TryGetValue), which is what let MorphologicalRule/Slot/AffixTemplate above be
     evidenced by an ordinary single flip instead. Do not read these four's own isActive="no" as
     evidenced by any word above; only the joint mutation with their dedicated partner can show it.

     Live declarations carry an explicit isActive="yes". That writes the DTD's own default, which the
     loader supplies anyway, so it exercises the value's presence in a document and NOT a behavioural
     difference.

     Decoys are deliberately never referenced by ID from an active declaration, so dropping one can
     never leave a dangling IDREF; the exceptions are slotDecoy (references mrDecoy, both inactive
     together) and mrBoundaryPfx (references the live bLive, by design; see above).

     Pure morphotactics: no PhonologicalFeatureSystem, bare SegmentDefinitions, one empty ncAny
     class used only inside MorphologicalInput stem patterns. -->
<HermitCrabInput>
  <Language isActive="yes">
    <Name>IsActiveBreadth</Name>
    <PartsOfSpeech>
      <PartOfSpeech id="posN"><Name>n</Name></PartOfSpeech>
    </PartsOfSpeech>
    <MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeatures>
      <MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeature id="mprLive" isActive="yes">Live</MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeature>
      <MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeature id="mprDecoy" isActive="no">Decoy</MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeature>
    </MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeatures>
    <CharacterDefinitionTable id="tableLive" isActive="yes">
      <Name>Live</Name>
      <SegmentDefinitions>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cA" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>a</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cE" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>e</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cO" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>o</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cU" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>u</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cK" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>k</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cL" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>l</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cM" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>m</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cP" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>p</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cQ" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>q</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cT" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>t</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <!-- Decoy: "z" is not a segment of this language, so any word containing it has no valid
             shape. A loader that ignored isActive here would let "zal" tokenize. -->
        <SegmentDefinition id="cZ" isActive="no"><Representations><Representation>z</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
      </SegmentDefinitions>
      <BoundaryDefinitions>
        <BoundaryDefinition id="bLive" isActive="yes"><Representations><Representation>+</Representation></Representations></BoundaryDefinition>
        <BoundaryDefinition id="bDecoy" isActive="no"><Representations><Representation>=</Representation></Representations></BoundaryDefinition>
      </BoundaryDefinitions>
    </CharacterDefinitionTable>
    <!-- Decoy table: never named by a Stratum's characterDefinitionTable, so an ignored isActive
         cannot change any parse. Present for the enumerated value on this element, not as a
         discriminating control. -->
    <CharacterDefinitionTable id="tableDecoy" isActive="no">
      <Name>Decoy</Name>
      <SegmentDefinitions>
        <SegmentDefinition id="dZ"><Representations><Representation>z</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
      </SegmentDefinitions>
    </CharacterDefinitionTable>
    <NaturalClasses>
      <FeatureNaturalClass id="ncAny" isActive="yes"><Name>Any</Name></FeatureNaturalClass>
      <FeatureNaturalClass id="ncDecoy" isActive="no"><Name>Decoy</Name></FeatureNaturalClass>
    </NaturalClasses>
    <Families>
      <Family id="famLive" isActive="yes">Live</Family>
      <Family id="famDecoy" isActive="no">Decoy</Family>
    </Families>
    <Strata>
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="tableLive" morphologicalRuleOrder="unordered" isActive="yes">
        <Name>Main</Name>
        <MorphologicalRuleDefinitions>
          <!-- Neither blockable nor multipleApplication is written here. Confirmed against the C#
               oracle that multipleApplication has NO observable effect on a rule reached through an
               AffixTemplate slot, which applies each slot once: with "2" set, "tatakul" still does
               not parse. Writing "1" would therefore have been the DTD default on an attribute this
               grammar cannot observe, which is coverage with nothing behind it. Both attributes stay
               in the coverage ledger as todo, for a fixture that applies a rule at stratum level
               where repetition is observable. "tatakul"/"tatatakul" remain as controls on the slot
               applying once. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrPfx" isActive="yes" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>livePrefix</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subPfx" isActive="yes">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemPfx"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>ta</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemPfx" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
              <!-- Decoy subrule of a LIVE rule: would prefix "mu" and give every stem a second
                   analysis. -->
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subPfxDecoy" isActive="no">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemPfxD"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>mu</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemPfxD" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
              <!-- Joint-mutation partner for FeatureNaturalClass/isActive/no: an inactive subrule of
                   the already-wired mrPfx, referencing the equally inactive ncDecoy. MorphologicalSubrule
                   is itself filtered by IsActive (XmlLanguageLoader.cs), so it never dereferences
                   ncDecoy while either stays inactive; activating both together is what a joint
                   counterfactual needs to test, without disturbing subPfx's own live analysis. -->
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subNcDecoy" isActive="no">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemNcDecoy"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncDecoy" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>ot</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemNcDecoy" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <MorphemeId>TA</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>TA</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- Decoy rule, reachable only from the equally inactive slotDecoy. Jointly inactive with
               its slot, so this pair alone cannot separate MorphologicalRule@isActive from
               Slot@isActive; see mrRuleOnly/mrSlotOnly below for the independently-reachable
               controls that do. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrDecoy" isActive="no" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>decoyPrefix</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subDecoy">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemDecoy"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>pe</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemDecoy" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <MorphemeId>PE</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>PE</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- Inactive rule reachable ONLY from an ACTIVE slot (ruleOnlySlot below). A Slot's own
               morphologicalRules IDREFS are resolved through a tolerant Dictionary.TryGetValue
               (XmlLanguageLoader.cs's LoadAffixTemplate), not the fail-fast indexer several other
               collections use, so an active slot naming an inactive rule loads fine with that rule
               simply absent from the slot; this is what makes MorphologicalRule@isActive
               separable from Slot@isActive at all. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrRuleOnly" isActive="no" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>ruleOnlyPrefix</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subRuleOnly">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemRuleOnly"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>qo</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemRuleOnly" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <MorphemeId>QO</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>QO</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- Active rule reachable ONLY from an INACTIVE slot (slotOnlySlot below). -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrSlotOnly" isActive="yes" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>slotOnlyPrefix</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subSlotOnly">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemSlotOnly"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>qe</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemSlotOnly" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <MorphemeId>QE</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>QE</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- Active rule reachable ONLY from decoyTemplate below (never from liveTemplate, and
               never stratum-listed), so activating that inactive TEMPLATE is what makes this rule
               reachable; unlike reusing mrPfx, which duplicates a result liveTemplate already
               produces and so is deduplicated away (Word implements value equality on its morphs and
               shape, so two derivations landing on the identical surface analysis collapse to one). -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrTemplateOnly" isActive="yes" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>templateOnlyPrefix</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subTemplateOnly">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemTemplateOnly"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>qu</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemTemplateOnly" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <MorphemeId>QU</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>QU</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- Live control for BoundaryDefinition@isActive: inserts the live boundary character "+"
               itself, not just segments adjacent to one, so deactivating bLive makes this rule's own
               output character unrecognized rather than merely affecting an unrelated word. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrBoundaryPfx" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>boundaryPrefix</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subBoundaryPfx">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemBoundaryPfx"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>mo</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>+</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments><CopyFromInput index="stemBoundaryPfx" /></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <MorphemeId>MO</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>MO</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
        </MorphologicalRuleDefinitions>
        <AffixTemplates>
          <AffixTemplate requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" isActive="yes" final="true">
            <Name>liveTemplate</Name>
            <Slot isActive="yes" optional="true" morphologicalRules="mrPfx"><Name>prefix</Name></Slot>
            <!-- Decoy slot: inactive, and the only route to the equally inactive mrDecoy. -->
            <Slot isActive="no" morphologicalRules="mrDecoy"><Name>decoySlot</Name></Slot>
            <!-- Independent control for MorphologicalRule@isActive: this slot is itself ACTIVE, so
                 whether it ever contributes anything depends solely on mrRuleOnly's own isActive. -->
            <Slot isActive="yes" optional="true" morphologicalRules="mrRuleOnly"><Name>ruleOnlySlot</Name></Slot>
            <!-- Independent control for Slot@isActive: mrSlotOnly is itself ACTIVE, so whether it is
                 ever reachable depends solely on this slot's own isActive. -->
            <Slot isActive="no" optional="true" morphologicalRules="mrSlotOnly"><Name>slotOnlySlot</Name></Slot>
            <Slot isActive="yes" optional="true" morphologicalRules="mrBoundaryPfx"><Name>boundarySlot</Name></Slot>
          </AffixTemplate>
          <!-- Decoy template: inactive, and its only slot reaches mrTemplateOnly, a rule with no
               other route (not stratum-listed, not in any other template's slot). A loader that
               ignored isActive here would newly admit "kulqu". -->
          <AffixTemplate requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" isActive="no">
            <Name>decoyTemplate</Name>
            <Slot optional="true" morphologicalRules="mrTemplateOnly"><Name>decoyTemplateSlot</Name></Slot>
          </AffixTemplate>
        </AffixTemplates>
        <LexicalEntries>
          <LexicalEntry id="eKul" isActive="yes" partOfSpeech="posN" family="famLive" ruleFeatures="mprLive" partial="false">
            <Allomorphs>
              <Allomorph id="aKul" isActive="yes" isBound="false"><PhoneticShape>kul</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
              <!-- Decoy allomorph of a LIVE entry: "kol" would be an accepted surface form. -->
              <Allomorph id="aKolDecoy" isActive="no"><PhoneticShape>kol</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
            </Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>KUL</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>vessel</Gloss>
            <Properties>
              <Property name="provenance">isActive breadth probe</Property>
              <Property name="note">Properties and Property are loaded but never exercised elsewhere</Property>
            </Properties>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- Decoy entry: "mep" would be an accepted root. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eMepDecoy" isActive="no" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs>
              <Allomorph id="aMepDecoy"><PhoneticShape>mep</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
            </Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>MEP</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>decoy</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- Joint-mutation partner for Family/isActive/no: an inactive entry naming the equally
               inactive famDecoy. LexicalEntry is filtered by IsActive before its own "family" IDREF is
               ever read, so this never dereferences famDecoy while either stays inactive. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eTupDecoy" isActive="no" partOfSpeech="posN" family="famDecoy">
            <Allomorphs>
              <Allomorph id="aTupDecoy"><PhoneticShape>tup</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
            </Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>TUP</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>tupdecoy</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- Joint-mutation partner for MorphologicalPhonologicalRuleFeature/isActive/no: an inactive
               entry naming the equally inactive mprDecoy via "ruleFeatures", same reasoning as
               eTupDecoy above. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="ePutDecoy" isActive="no" partOfSpeech="posN" ruleFeatures="mprDecoy">
            <Allomorphs>
              <Allomorph id="aPutDecoy"><PhoneticShape>put</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
            </Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>PUT</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>putdecoy</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
        </LexicalEntries>
      </Stratum>
      <!-- Decoy stratum: its root "qot" would be an accepted word. -->
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="tableLive" morphologicalRuleOrder="unordered" isActive="no">
        <Name>DecoyStratum</Name>
        <LexicalEntries>
          <LexicalEntry id="eQotDecoy" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs>
              <Allomorph id="aQotDecoy"><PhoneticShape>qot</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
            </Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>QOT</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>decoy</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
        </LexicalEntries>
      </Stratum>
      <!-- Joint-mutation partner for CharacterDefinitionTable/isActive/no: an inactive Stratum whose
           own characterDefinitionTable IDREF names the equally inactive tableDecoy. Stratum is itself
           filtered by IsActive before that IDREF is ever read (XmlLanguageLoader.LoadStratum), so this
           never dereferences tableDecoy while either stays inactive; activating both together is what
           a joint counterfactual needs. The entry's root "z" reuses tableDecoy's own (always-active)
           segment, so nothing new needs declaring there.
           Must stay the LAST Stratum: Language.SurfaceStratum (Language.cs) is the last entry in
           Strata, and Morpher.cs tokenizes every input word through ONLY that stratum's
           CharacterDefinitionTable; there is no other way for tableDecoy to ever tokenize anything.
           A confirmed, unavoidable consequence, not a bug: the joint mutant that activates this pair
           therefore also reassigns which table tokenizes "kul"/"takul"/etc, so their status changes
           too, in addition to "z" gaining a real parse. That collateral change is still honest
           evidence (a delta is a delta); it just is not scoped to "z" alone the way the other three
           joint pairs in this fixture are. -->
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="tableDecoy" morphologicalRuleOrder="unordered" isActive="no">
        <Name>TableDecoyStratum</Name>
        <LexicalEntries>
          <LexicalEntry id="eZDecoy" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs>
              <Allomorph id="aZDecoy"><PhoneticShape>z</PhoneticShape></Allomorph>
            </Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>ZD</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>zdecoy</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
        </LexicalEntries>
      </Stratum>
    </Strata>
  </Language>
  <!-- Decoy Language. The loader selects the single ACTIVE Language
       (XmlLanguageLoader.cs, Elements("Language").Single(IsActive)), so ignoring isActive here makes
       Single throw and the whole grammar fail to load. The strongest control in this fixture. -->
  <Language isActive="no">
    <Name>DecoyLanguage</Name>
    <PartsOfSpeech>
      <PartOfSpeech id="posDecoy"><Name>x</Name></PartOfSpeech>
    </PartsOfSpeech>
    <CharacterDefinitionTable id="tableDecoyLang">
      <Name>DecoyLang</Name>
      <SegmentDefinitions>
        <SegmentDefinition id="dqZ"><Representations><Representation>z</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
      </SegmentDefinitions>
    </CharacterDefinitionTable>
    <Strata>
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="tableDecoyLang">
        <Name>DecoyLangStratum</Name>
      </Stratum>
    </Strata>
  </Language>
</HermitCrabInput>
